  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge pattern continues Friday which will bring \nanother day of hot temperatures to the region. Drier PWATs and \nlittle forcing Friday will keep most of the region dry, but I can't \nrule out a few showers and thunderstorms in Southwest MT during \nthe afternoon. Temperatures peak on Saturday as the ridge \nbreakdowns and southwest flow increases. Lower elevations are \nforecasted to reach into the 100s across north-Central MT and the \nupper 90s in Southwest MT. The ridge breakdown also brings an \nincrease in 700mb winds through Sunday, peaking late Saturday \nnight into early Sunday morning to 60-70kts along the Northern \nRocky Mountain Front. As a result, strong winds develop along the \nRocky Mountain Front windy conditions develop elsewhere Saturday \nand Sunday. This with the combination of hot temperatures and low \nrelative humidity creates concerns for elevated to critical fire \nweather conditions (see FIRE WEATHER section). \n\nA mostly dry frontal passage Sunday will help bring a relief in \nthe heat through the first half of next week. Northwest flow \naloft will help keep temperatures down through Tuesday, before \nvery warm temperatures return. Low moisture throughout the week \nwill keep conditions dry.  -Wilson\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWeekend Heat:\n\nModel agreement remains high on the potential for widespread high\ntemperatures in the upper 90s to upper 100s on Saturday. The\nprevious Extreme Heat Watches have now been upgraded to Extreme\nHeat Warnings with additional Heat Advisories added for\nsurrounding areas.\n\nCascade county and the lower elevations of Judith Basin county \nwill get hot during the day but manage to cool off. Chouteau and \nFergus counties along with the Bears Paw zone will be more at risk\nfor maintaining the warm overnight lows which will provide \nadditional heat stress. Thus the Cascade and Judith Basin warnings\nwill end earlier while the Chouteau, Fergus, and Bears Paw \nwarnings will continue until around the time the cooler air from \nthe front is expected to reach those zones. The Gallatin and \nMadison Valley zones were added to the Extreme Heat Warning based \non the expected highs compared to climatology. Both Bozeman and \nEnnis are at risk for having a top 10 high temperature event on \nSaturday which means this kind of heat is generally on the rare \nside for these areas and may have greater impacts for both\nresidents and those recreating in the area. Given that these two \nvalleys will cool off Sunday morning, these warnings will also \ndrop off earlier compared to the North-Central Montana zones.\n\nSupplemental Heat Advisories were issued for surrounding areas to\nbring attention to locations that might not get as hot or have as\nsignificant impacts but still be a concern for those who are\nwithout air conditioning or plan on spending a significant amount\nof time outdoors.\n\n\nStrong Winds Saturday/Sunday:\n\nThere is still strong model agreement in strong winds developing\nover the Rocky Mountain Front Saturday afternoon through Sunday\nmorning with the strongest winds expected between midnight and 8\nAM Sunday. With these winds being well outside of climatology for\nthis time of year, the potential for impacts to those recreating\nalong the Rocky Mountain Front, and the overall confidence in the\nevent occurring, the decision was made to upgrade the watch to a\nHigh Wind Warning. Additionally, with there being strong hints in\nthe models that the front could arrive a little earlier, the High\nWind Warning will start at noon on Saturday to account for this\npossibility.\n\nAcross the rest of the region gusts between 35 and 50 mph are\npossible with most locations outside the warning unlikely to reach\nhigh wind criteria. But there still will be a concern for those\ntraveling along north-south roads as locally stronger crosswinds\nin some of the typical wind-prone areas may make travel difficult\nfor high profile vehicles and those towing trailers.  -thor",
